title: LSP Semantic Tokens - (Example)

\procedure lsp.st.greet(who)
<$let lsp.st.mood="happy">Hello <<who>>, <<lsp.st.mood>> at <<now>></$let>
\end

Open this tiddler's `.tid` file in your editor. With VS Code's defaults, core names are italic and definitions bold. Turn the `tiddlywiki.lsp.semanticColors` settings on one by one to see each kind of name in colour.

<<lsp.st.greet who:"you">>

<$list filter="[tag[LSP Capabilities]limit[1]] [caption[x]]" variable="lsp.st.item"> <<lsp.st.item>></$list>

|! Look at |! Sent as |
|`lsp.st.greet` in the first line |''function'', declaration: bold |
|`who` in the first line |''parameter'', declaration: bold |
|`<<who>>` in the second line |''parameter'' |
|`<<lsp.st.mood>>` |''variable'' |
|`<<now>>` |''macro'', core: italic |
|`<<lsp.st.greet` below the text |''function'' |
|`$list` |''class'', core: italic |
|`tag` and `limit` |''method'', core: italic |
|`caption` |''property'': no operator has that name, so ~TiddlyWiki tests the field |
|`<<lsp.st.item>>` |''variable'' |

